Subject: [PATCH 3/3] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 4, patch 3;
 built on f9e70c167 with series 2 and 3 applied): THE ANCHOR OF THE LAST
 REVIEW IS THE COMMIT IN WHICH THAT REVIEW LANDED (found on main's f9e70c167
 while reading main's page on the periodic review). The count of landings
 since the last review was taken from the last 'anchor-commit:' anywhere on
 the reviews' page. One sentence at the end of that page, naming the head as
 an anchor, made the count 0, and review-fires, review-core, review-actions
 and carry-age all passed: the block that holds until the review is run was
 switched off by a line of prose. On main the anchor is never free: each of
 the last eight reviews has one anchor, on a line of its own, and it names the
 commit in which the review's entry landed. THE MEND reads exactly that,
 before the count is taken: the entry of the last review names an anchor once,
 on its own line, and nothing after it on the page names another; the anchor
 is the commit in which that entry's heading landed, read from history; and no
 older entry stands below it. Where one of these does not hold, review-fires
 fails with the sentence. It binds from Review 78 on: older entries are in
 other forms and are not re-judged; a page with no such entry, or a test's own
 text, is read as before. What it does not reach: a whole entry that is made
 up, heading and core lines and anchor, which is a review that was not run and
 not a slip. The review's own script reads its anchor as before; with the gate
 red on a moved anchor, a push is blocked. The lock:
 tests/test_the_reviews_anchor_is_the_commit_it_landed_in.py, six tests, each
 in a small repository where a review is landed as main lands one; on the
 commit before, five fail, three of them because the gate reads '1 merges
 since the last review'. Computed once; tested against planted errors, 4 of 4.
 0 of 19.

